            <ArticleTitle>Translocation across a self-healing block copolymer membrane.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>6689-6693</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1039/c7sm01284e</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Herein, a membrane prepared from the self-assembly of poly(styrene-co-acrylonitrile)-b-poly(ethylene oxide)-b-poly(styrene-co-acrylonitrile) micelles is found to exhibit translocation of nano-objects dispersed in aqueous solution. With the water flow as a driving force, temporary pores are created in between the micelles to facilitate the passage of nano-objects. These temporary pores close afterwards through a self-healing mechanism. As main results, polystyrene and silica nanoparticles exhibited a selective translocation directly influenced by their size and applied pressure.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
